  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: Implementation of CAPTA Requirements for Substance-Affected Infants**\n\nThe report examines the implementation of the Child Abuse Prevention and Treatment Act (CAPTA) requirements for substance-affected infants. CAPTA provides formula grants to states to improve child protective service systems, and states are required to submit plans outlining how they intend to use CAPTA funds to improve their child protective service systems.\n\nThe report highlights several challenges states face in implementing CAPTA requirements, including:\n\n1. **Notification of Substance-Affected Infants**: States struggle to define which substance-affected infants health care providers are required to notify Child Protective Services (CPS) about, and how to implement this requirement.\n2. **Development of Plans of Safe Care**: States face challenges in developing plans of safe care for substance-affected infants, including defining what a plan of safe care is and for whom it should be developed.\n3. **Data Collection**: States struggle to collect data on substance-affected infants, including the number of notifications received from health care providers and the number of plans of safe care developed.\n\nThe report also highlights the importance of providing adequate supports and services to substance-affected infants to ensure their safety. The report recommends that the Secretary of Health and Human Services (HHS) direct the Administration for Children and Families (ACF) to provide additional guidance and technical assistance to states to address these challenges.\n\nKey findings of the report include:\n\n* 42 states require health care providers to notify CPS about substance-affected infants.\n* 49 states have policies to develop plans of safe care for substance-affected infants.\n* 38 states reported that additional guidance on requirements for health care providers to notify CPS about substance-affected infants would be extremely or very helpful.\n* 37 states reported that additional guidance on developing, implementing, and monitoring plans of safe care would be extremely or very helpful.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for additional guidance and technical assistance to states to ensure effective implementation of CAPTA requirements for substance-affected infants.",
